Pictures were an afterthought.  The ability for hyperlinks to point
to anything, on any site, was really the key thing that made the
web better than gopher.  You could use web pages to index all
your other content -- newsgroups, gopher content, ftp, other web
pages, even telnet services could all be aggregated, according to
a user-centric topic criterion, and linked from one web page.  This
is why the web became the first port of call and thus took over
pretty much the whole internet.

Pictures?  You could put pictures on an ftp server, and anyone
could get them who wanted them.  But people couldn't open an
ftp client, point it to your ftp site about Spam, and then click on
three links to open rec.food.spam in a newsreader, download
a Spam rating application from ftp.sunsite.net/spam, and read
hierarchically organized information about Spam from gopher.
For that you needed a web page.

Of course, once you *have* a website about Spam, you
might as well go ahead and put the hierarchical information
on the website, so gopher quickly became obsolete -- the
web obviated it.  You could stick the photos on the website
too, while you were at it.  Later, when HTTP was enhanced
to support resuming a partially completed download, you
could distribute your application via the website, and ftp
became obsolete (except maybe for webmasters to use to
upload their content to the web server).

Usenet held on for a long time, because newsreaders are
very featureful, but make no mistake:  eventually the web
will completely displace it.  It's just taking a lot longer.
Web-based fora, although they've existed for a long time,
were *severely* impractical until the advent of several
relatively recent browser technologies (not least, tabbed
browsing with session-store so you don't lose all your
tabs if a plugin crashes).  Even now, the limitations of
the text-editing capabilities the major browsers support
for textarea fields are problematic at best.  But in time
that will change too.  Already there are only a spare
handful of newsreaders that support all of the editing
features a browser textarea does (e.g., spellcheck).
Gnus is one example, but it's not suitable for most
users, on account of the learning curve. (To really
get comfortable with it, you need to learn lisp.)  So
far, although things like spellcheck are useful, the
major browsers' textarea fields are still missing
some rather key features (not least, the ability to
correctly re-wrap nested quotes; any newsreader
worth a dime can do this).  But they're getting better...
Eventually, the web will subsume usenet.  For a
lot of users, it already has:  for a lot of subjects,
web-based discussion fora are where everything
is being said.  It's a gradual process, but it's not
stopping.

Email is taking even longer.  I think the percentage
of email users who only ever use a web interface to
get their mail is, what, 30% now?  35%?  It's not a
majority, but it does keep increasing.  Also, most
web-based email still uses standard email protocols
under the hood -- but social networking sites are
starting to change that; I have family members now
who "email" one another on a social website that I
shall not name, because it's a site I don't care to
promote (but if you must know, its Alexa rank is 2).
This is for now still a minority use case, but it's
growing...

I'm pretty sure the web will eventually take over the
VOIP market too.  All browsers really need to add at
this point is the ability to pull audio/visual data from
an input peripheral, and somebody will make a video
chat website, a sort of a cross between YouTube
and Skype.  The first one will be laggy and bogged
down in advertising and bad UI, but competitors will
come along...

And wasn't somebody just a couple of weeks ago
talking about wanting to create a web-based UI for
a NetHack variant?

The web is like the borg.  Resistance is futile.  All
your directly user-visible network services will be
assimilated.  (Under the hood, of course, there will
always still be DNS and ntp and various other
infrastructure protocols.  For that matter, some
web sites have plugins on them that use other
protocols under the hood.)


